# ord-named-v1 — named-certificate-slot policy for worked example S6
#
# The strict leg of S2's ord-v1, alone. One rule, certified by the registered
# ord@1 backend (src/Lara/Strict/Ord.hs, lean/Lara/Ord.lean): the bare ordered
# comparison over two reported score cells, re-checked in exact rational
# arithmetic from a certificate naming its two consulted premise slots.
#
# S6's subject — the lara-syntax@0.6 symbolic premise-slot spelling
# (`(prem <name>)`, #105) — lives entirely in the ARTIFACT's certificate
# payload. Nothing about it reaches this file: a policy declares which
# certifier a strict rule accepts and which theory its certificates cite,
# and both declarations below are declared the same way ord-v1 declares
# them (the theory digest is necessarily this policy's own).
# That is the point of pairing this policy with S6's artifact: #105 changed
# the authoring surface only, so the policy vocabulary is untouched.
#
# No bridge rule, no measurand, no comparison-scheme: S2 carries the §3.6
# layering story (strict arithmetic, defeasible bridge), and S6 deliberately
# does not re-tell it. Here the claim IS the bare comparison, so the policy
# needs only the rule that certifies it.

policy ord-named-v1

# --- Signature (spec §2, §3.4; lara-core@0.2, issue #89) -------------------------------------------
# The many-sorted proposition signature the checker enforces as rejection class R2 at stage 2:
# every predicate and constructor symbol this policy's rules and programs may use, with the sort
# of each argument position. `Num` and `Str` are the two built-in base sorts (the sorts of numeric
# and string literals) and are not declarable.
#
# Generated by `scripts/infer-sigma.hs` (union-find over argument occurrences, finest consistent
# partition), then the sorts were given their intended names — the one editorial step, and the
# only one:
#
#   cabal exec -- runghc --ghc-arg=-package --ghc-arg=lara scripts/infer-sigma.hs \
#     examples/S6/ord-named-v1.policy.lara examples/S6/example.lara

sort Experiment, Cell, System, Measurand, Dataset

con accuracy : Measurand
con exp1 : Experiment
con imagenet_val : Dataset
con score_cell(System, Measurand, Dataset, Num) : Cell
con sys_base : System
con sys_new : System

pred num_lt(Num, Num)
pred reports(Experiment, Cell)

# --- The certificate-checked ordered comparison ----------------------------------------------
# S2's beats_recheck with the same mode, premises, conclusion, and certifier backend; only the
# rule name and this policy's theory digest differ. Slot order is the premise list
# order, and the certificate cites it: slot 0 is the baseline cell, slot 1 the system cell.
# What S6's artifact adds is only HOW those two slots are cited — by the leaf names that fill
# them — and that spelling is gone before this rule's certificate is ever replayed.
#
# Both compared values trace to a consulted premise (design §2.4): a threshold or public
# baseline is not a bare goal constant, it enters as a leaf feeding a premise slot.

rule lt_recheck(S, B, Q, D, Exp, Sv, Bv)
  mode       = strict
  premises   = [ reports(Exp, score_cell(B, Q, D, Bv)),
                 reports(Exp, score_cell(S, Q, D, Sv)) ]
  conclusion = num_lt(Bv, Sv)
  allow-trusted = false
  certifiers = [ (ord@1, sha256:ord-named-v1-theory-0) ]

# The empty theory the ord@1 certificates cite: the certified comparison is pure arithmetic, so
# the theory contributes no entries — the digest is selection identity only (the S1 precedent).
# Emptiness here is a property of THIS policy, which the .lara door pins; it is not something the
# backend enforces. What ord@1 enforces is that theory entries are UNCITABLE (design §2.2): a unit
# may declare entries and still be accepted, but no certificate can consult one.
theory sha256:ord-named-v1-theory-0 = []

# WELL-FORMEDNESS (spec §8.1, Path B): the only strict rule is lt_recheck, whose conclusion
# pattern num_lt(Bv, Sv) heads no contrary pair, so no contrary side is strict-reachable and the
# policy passes the R12 check — the same argument, and the same Lean lemmas, as ord-v1.
